ABSTRACT

The Adhesion–Cohesion Astrophysical Simulation Engine (ACASE‑v9.8) introduces a novel framework for cosmic structure formation by integrating adhesion force laws, cohesion tensor fields, and encrypted manifold evolution into a unified GPU‑parallel architecture. Unlike conventional ΛCDM simulations relying solely on gravitational N‑body dynamics and hydrodynamic pressure, ACASE‑v9.8 models material‑like bonding behaviors that generate early, smooth, and stable filamentary structures; membrane‑like baryonic sheets; and sharply bounded dark matter halos. A dynamic SHA‑512‑based cipher seals each manifold state, producing a tamper‑proof, self‑verifying simulation environment. Preliminary results suggest the existence of previously unmodeled bonding regimes in large‑scale cosmic evolution and demonstrate the feasibility of encrypted physics engines as a new class of scientific architecture.

